fix: build the Gradle functional tests against the version under test

Passing vaadin.version through env(1) on the gradlew command line does
not work: Gradle drops environment variables whose names are not valid
identifiers, so the variable never reaches the build JVM, let alone the
test workers. A probe in the build script shows System.getenv() has no
such key even though the same variable is visible to bash and to a plain
java process started the same way.

Set it on the functionalTest task instead, from the version being built.
That version already comes from the root pom, which CI rewrites to
999.99-SNAPSHOT, so the generated test projects now resolve com.vaadin:flow
from the workspace the job restores and the workflow needs no plumbing of
its own. -Pvaadin.version still overrides both, as before.

AbstractGradleTest keeps a guard for the case where that wiring is
removed or renamed: the fallback to the published snapshot is silent, so
without it the functional tests would go on passing while testing a
released version rather than the branch. It now fails when CI is set and
the variable is missing, and the resolved version is printed for every
test so the log says what was actually built against.
